{"conjecture": "Long-running software systems degrade not through any single flawed change, but through the silent accumulation of implicit dependencies between modules that were never designed to interact. Each locally-correct modification slightly tightens this hidden coupling graph, and because the coupling is emergent rather than encoded, tests and reviews calibrated on prior system states cannot detect it. The system becomes harder to change because the true interaction surface of any component has grown far beyond its explicit interface, making the blast radius of modifications unpredictable."}
